    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d59">

                <pb facs="0179" n="91" sameAs="frus:frus1904/pg91"/>

                <head><hi rendition="#italic">Mr. Hay to Mr.
                            Storer.</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Department of State</hi>,<lb/><hi rendition="#italic"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1904-12-14"><hi rendition="#italic">December 14,
                        1904</hi></date>.</dateline>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 122.]</seg></opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: Referring to the instructions of this date
                    in connection with the case of Mrs. John Horniak and similar cases, I inclose a
                    copy of a letter from Thomas Capek, esq., of 300 East Seventy-second street, New
                    York City, submitting the complaint of Mrs. Sophia Vanco (otherwise Wancho) of
                    treatment received at the hands of Hungarian officials, the seizure of her
                    ticket, etc.<anchor n="a" xml:id="d59-f1"/></p>
                <p>You will, in addition to the cases of similar character already transmitted to
                    the foreign office, present Mrs. Vanco’s case, with a view to a thorough inquiry
                    into the causes of these complaints and to suitable restitution and
                    reimbursement, as pointed out in the Department’s instruction in connection with
                    the case of Mrs. Maria Horniak, above referred to.</p>
                <p>I am, etc.,</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">John Hay.</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
